ed{...}.\n\nIf $a$ and $b$ are the solutions to the equation $x^{2} - 5x + 9= 0$, what is the value of $(a - 1)(b - 1)$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"607a21a237e617e6204d7125be6e361ca3852a114a50a17a46b2046ec7043d8f"},"seconds":6.517366886138916,"seed":448539341260529559,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by identifying the roots \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 5x + 9 = 0\\). According to Vieta's formulas, for a quadratic equation of the form \\(x^2 + px + q = 0\\), the sum of the roots \\(a + b\\) is given by \\(-p\\) and the product of the roots \\(ab\\) is given by \\(q\\). Here, \\(p = -5\\) and \\(q = 9\\), so we have:\n\n\\[a + b = 5\\]\n\\[ab = 9\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\((a - 1)(b - 1)\\). We can expand this expression as follows:\n\n\\[\n(a - 1)(b - 1) = ab - a - b + 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\(a + b\\) and \\(ab\\) from Vieta's formulas, we get:\n\n\\[\n(a - 1)(b - 1) = 9 - 5 + 1 = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\((a - 1)(b - 1)\\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:28"}
